how to compensate people when you don't  have any money so I got a great question  recently for one of my Twitter followers  love you guys in rich who's who's been a  great fan for a while and i really love  all this up he's saying so thank you in  rich his question was so I have an idea  it's going to be split between an owner  and development team what is the best  practice to split the percentage between  these two if the owner has no money this  is a great question and no one that a  lot of people struggle with and I did  too having built a few technology  companies and having a team and having  to split your percentages there's no  finite 1answer fits all solution but let  me give you some of the things to think  about so people obviously have to be  paid right people obviously have to be  paid for the time that they're putting  in feel like they're getting compensated  and it's pretty common that in a  start-up you don't have cash to pay out  right you know I have a rich uncle  giving you the cash to launch this thing  so the three most common ways one you do  a revenue share to you do a proper chair  and three you give up equity in the  business and I'll go through each one of  them quickly to give you a sense of what  you can do so the first one is a revenue  share and this is where you're giving a  percentage off the top of every sale  that comes in from your business so I  don't know what you're making I'm  assuming is some kind of software every  time that software is sold they get a  percentage off the top of the revenue do  those five percent me that's ten percent  whatever it is depends on your margins  right usually software has pretty good  margin so you can afford a higher number  but depending on what you're making if  you don't have the margins you can't  support a high revenue sharing number  this is great in that it doesn't give up  equity in your business and they get  their money coming in faster soon as  sales start happening they start making  money which can be a good incentive for  them one thing you might consider doing  is a sliding scale this is something  I've done a number of times with people  who are partnered up with in my business  so my business now I'm the only order  one hundred percent of the shares I  don't any partners but I do a lot of  joint ventures with different people and  often is based on a revenue share what I  understand is I want people working who  who are committed to this who want it to  be great who want to pour their hearts  into it but it's hard for them to do  that when they're earning ten percent of  the money that comes in and at the start  is not a lot of money coming in people  need to earn a wage right not making  tons of money but they need to earn  something to pay the bills put food on  the table and so on so what I'll often  do is a sliding scale and with my  products and services and the programs i  launched it may look like this for  example the goal is to get you to ten  percent of all of our sales so that's  ten percent i can i can afford that in  my margins long term i want this to be a  you know million-dollar your company  that's going to be one hundred thousand  dollars a year plus for you as we build  this thing up well I recognize that at  the start you know this is going to take  work you're not gonna make money right  away you need to earn a wage so you're  going to get fifty percent of the sales  up front so until you earn X amount you  figure out what is a livable wage until  you get to that point you are in half of  the sales and then after that it slides  down to ten percent so it gives them an  incentive to work harder right away  you're losing a little bit amaro to  obviously at the start but you're giving  them a livable way to keep them  motivated to incentivize they don't to  go back and get a job and then  longer-term it still works out down to  that ten percent or whatever you think  is reasonable with your business the  second is a profit share and with a  profit share it's a little bit harder  because you're tracking all the way down  to the bottom line not just the top line  it makes them start to think more like a  business owner makes them start thinking  about where you're spending the money  instead of just how to make more money  it gives them a little bit more  knowledge of how to run a business and  all the things to think about but it  means the money's going to come in a lot  slower right you may not make any profit  for the  first gear and can they go one year  without making any money right some  people can do that a lot of people can't  you know they don't have the statements  they can't build that up but that's  another option to go if you can say  we're going to give you x percent of the  profits as we build out you know that's  a little bit harder to project you don't  quite know how profitable you're going  to be and you have to be i would say for  going that route to to be flexible you  can have a plan but be flexible because  it's probably going to change as you  start getting into its services and  sales the third one is give up piece of  the company and this is what most people  think of you know i'm giving up x  percent of my company not just my  profits or my revenues but shares and  what you're willing to think about is do  I want this person to be a part of my  business and owned shares is this person  think like a business person because  it's really hard to get somebody out of  a company and just so much trouble and  headache like it's like getting into a  marriage or you want to make sure it's  the right person you're gonna spend a  lot of time with this person you better  get along you better have the same  values and vision for what you want to  think and happen with the business so  with the shares there's no right or  wrong answer okay at the end of the day  it's going to come down to what you're  both happy with but some of the things  to think about are one what does each  person bring to the table so you're the  owner you're the new the idea guy you're  the creator what you need development  helps is worse coming on and being a  developer for you they might say hey you  know this two of us so 50-50 split makes  sense to me but you may think hey you  know what's my idea I'm you just a  programmer I'm going to be out selling  this saying I need to bring on more  people I should all ninety percent you  own ten percent right so somewhere there  you have to find common ground but it  starts was thinking about who's bringing  what to the table right and what are you  going to need to grow this into a real  business the program isn't part of it  but what else do they bring or do you  bring and the whole needs analysis to  build a solid company you know who's  bringing what to the table the other  thing to think about is what has already  gone into this business  if you've already put a lot of time  effort energy ideas dollars into this  company and they're coming in brand new  then that has to count for part of it  right so when I joined my first offer  company I wasn't the founder I joined  two or three years into the company I  became an owner in the business but I  wasn't a full equal owner was an equal  partner I had a little bit less than my  other two founders because they had put  a couple years work into when I was  joining brand new when i started my next  software company i started with a  partner we had 50 50 each and then we  brought in the new person and made them  earn in they started maybe six months  after and so we made I made that person  for an in and he could earn up to a  maximum of i think twenty five or thirty  percent of the shares remember what it  was we wanted new person earning and  show that he was a good fit for the team  regardless of what you do you should  have something to protect yourself you  should have some kind of agreement so if  it's the proper to revenue share that  there's an easy way to cancel out of it  either of you can cancel whatever the  penalties are have that written down in  the contract so you're not stuck with  this person for a long time if it  doesn't work out if they're not  programming or if you guys just don't  get along anymore and on a shareholders  agreement that become part of your  company make sure you have a shotgun  clause which basically means that  somebody's going to buy each other out  if you trigger a shotgun clause you  could say i'm going to buy your shares  for this amount of money or if you say  no you can buy my shares for that same  valuation and to make sure that one of  you is going to be out of the business  but you have to have something like that  in just so that you're not stuck forever  with a partner who doesn't work out I  know it's it's challenging it's tough  and each negotiation is its own bear  that you wrestle with but hopefully that  helped shed a little bit of light if you  do revenue share profit share or send it  to the company and then help them figure  it down to what percentage you're going  to give up for those are you watching I  love to know how you've done deals in  the past  leave a comment below let me know if you  have a business part now how you split  it up if you've done revenue shares and  so on i love to hear from you and add  some more contacts if you want to leave  a comment if you like this video give it  a thumbs up and you want to see more  like this click on my face subscribe to  the channel thank you so much I'll see  you soon
 

